<BaseLayout
  title="Beta-Alanine: Dosing & Tingling Explained — Nawtch"
  description="ISSN-backed dosing for beta-alanine and high-intensity performance, plus why it causes harmless tingling (paresthesia) and how to reduce it."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/beta-alanine/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Beta-Alanine</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Helps with short, high-intensity efforts, and comes with a harmless tingling side effect most people notice.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/">← Back to Supplements</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Not medical advice — the tingling this supplement causes is expected and harmless, but talk to a doctor before starting if you're on other medications or have a health condition.</p>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Beta-alanine is an amino acid the body combines with histidine to form carnosine, which is stored in
        muscle and helps buffer the acid (hydrogen ions) that builds up during intense, sustained effort — the
        burn you feel during a hard set or sprint. Raising muscle carnosine through beta-alanine supplementation
        can delay that buildup slightly, which is why its benefits show up specifically in high-intensity efforts
        lasting roughly one to four minutes, not in short one-rep max lifts or long, steady endurance work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Position stand:</strong> Trexler ET, Smith-Ryan AE, Stout JR, et al. "International Society of
        Sports Nutrition Position Stand: Beta-Alanine," <em>Journal of the International Society of Sports
        Nutrition</em>, 2015. Concludes 4–6g/day for at least 2–4 weeks reliably raises muscle carnosine content
        and improves performance in high-intensity exercise, most consistently for efforts in the 1–4 minute
        range; it's considered safe at recommended doses, with paresthesia (tingling) as its only notable side
        effect.
      </p>
      <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4501114/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the position stand →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>The tingling, explained:</strong> the tingling sensation (paresthesia) some people feel after
        taking beta-alanine comes from it activating a specific sensory nerve receptor in the skin. It's
        transient — typically starting within 10–20 minutes and resolving within about an hour — and dose
        dependent, meaning a larger single dose produces a stronger sensation. It's considered harmless, and the
        ISSN position stand notes it can be reduced by splitting the daily dose into smaller amounts (roughly
        1.6g at a time) or using a sustained-release formulation, rather than taking the full daily dose at once.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Typical Dosing</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        4–6g/day, split into smaller doses of around 1.6g to minimize tingling, sustained for at least 2–4 weeks
        to build up muscle carnosine — this is a supplement that works cumulatively over time, not as an
        immediate pre-workout effect. Source: ISSN position stand, above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The tingling is harmless but can be uncomfortable for some people — splitting doses or using a
        sustained-release product reduces it. Beyond that, the ISSN position stand reports no serious safety
        concerns at recommended doses in the available research; as with any supplement, anyone with an existing
        health condition or on other medications should check with a doctor first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4501114/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISSN Position Stand — Beta-Alanine</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
